content:
{
  "email": "sofia.rossi@brightline.example",
  "reason": "KB-012 quick check: see if the meeting invite was quarantined"
}

{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Sofia, sorry you missed the Q4 planning invite. I checked your account and mailbox: everything is active, your mailbox has plenty of space, and the invite isn't in your spam quarantine, so it wasn't blocked as spam. A few things will help me narrow it down: 1) Please open Outlook on the web and check whether the invite or the meeting shows up on your calendar there. If it's on the web but not in the desktop app, that's a sync issue and we can fix it. 2) Does anyone else, such as an assistant or a coworker, have delegate access to your calendar? If so, invites might be going only to them. 3) Who organized the meeting? Is it someone inside Brightline or an outside contact? Have you had trouble getting other invites from this person before? In the meantime, the quickest fix is usually to ask the organizer to send the invite to you again. Let me know what you find and I'll take it from there.",
  "internal_note": "Ticket from sender's own address. User ID 7, Sofia Rossi, Marketing, not VIP. Account is active with no lockout. Mailbox is 27.9% used. Device BL-LT-0107. Following KB-012: the spam quarantine for this mailbox is empty, so the invite was not quarantined. I have no tool to check delegate settings, so I asked the user about delegates. I also asked her to compare Outlook on the web with the desktop app (KB-009 applies if only the desktop app is wrong) and to tell us who the organizer is and whether they're internal. I suggested asking the organizer to resend. If invites from one specific internal user never arrive, escalate to Tier 2 for a mail rule or transport check, per KB-012.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-012"
  ],
  "confidence": "medium"
}
